  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple schematic of a nuclear collision and the production of a hypernucleus. On the left, before impact, an incoming projectile nucleus moves toward a target nucleus. Both are shown as compact clusters of nucleons.\n\nOn the right, after the collision, the target appears to break into a target fragment, while a dense central region labeled as a hot participant zone emits particles outward. From this interaction zone, a hyperon is produced and then captured by the outgoing projectile-side fragment. That fragment is labeled as a projectile fragment, or hypernucleus, and it contains an added green particle representing the captured hyperon within the cluster of ordinary nucleons.\n\nThe overall message is that a high-energy nucleus–nucleus collision can create strange matter in the participant zone, and one of these strange particles can become bound to a surviving nuclear fragment. The image emphasizes the contrast between intact nuclei before the event and several distinct products after it: a target fragment, an excited participant region, and a projectile fragment transformed into a hypernucleus. The arrows indicate both the original beam direction and the post-collision emission and capture process.",
